Assistant: All examples pass, 500 randomized tests match brute force, and the worst case (16 requests) runs in 0.74s — well within the 10s limit.

**Approach:** Since `len(requests) <= 16`, any optimal schedule corresponds to an ordering of requests (a request fulfilled "in passing" can simply be inserted into the ordering at that point). For an ordering, fulfillment time follows `t = max(t + dist(prev_floor, floor_j), arrival_j)`. I use Held-Karp style DP over subsets: `dp[mask][last]` = earliest time to fulfill all requests in `mask` while ending at request `last`'s floor, with transitions relaxing all not-yet-fulfilled requests. Complexity `O(2^m · m²)`.

```python
class Solution:
    def elevatorRequests(self, n: int, start: int, requests: list[list[int]]) -> int:
        m = len(requests)
        arr = [r[0] for r in requests]
        fl = [r[1] for r in requests]

        # Pairwise distances between request floors
        D = [[0] * m for _ in range(m)]
        for i in range(m):
            fi = fl[i]
            Di = D[i]
            for j in range(m):
                Di[j] = abs(fi - fl[j])

        size = 1 << m
        full = size - 1
        INF = 1 << 62

        # dp[mask][last] = earliest time to fulfill all requests in mask,
        # ending positioned at requests[last]'s floor (last fulfilled stop).
        dp = [[INF] * m for _ in range(size)]
        for j in range(m):
            t = abs(fl[j] - start)
            if t < arr[j]:
                t = arr[j]
            dp[1 << j][j] = t

        for mask in range(size):
            rem = full ^ mask
            if rem == 0:
                continue
            row = dp[mask]
            for last in range(m):
                t = row[last]
                if t == INF:
                    continue
                drow = D[last]
                r = rem
                while r:
                    b = r & -r
                    j = b.bit_length() - 1
                    nt = t + drow[j]
                    if nt < arr[j]:
                        nt = arr[j]
                    nrow = dp[mask | b]
                    if nt < nrow[j]:
                        nrow[j] = nt
                    r ^= b

        return min(dp[full])
```